    "abstract": "Urban space is not equally accessible to all city inhabitants. For individuals with mobility disabilities, physically open spaces can remain functionally inaccessible, as inadequate sidewalk surfaces, excessive slopes, or insufficient clearance can turn a seemingly traversable area into an impassable one. Although the need to quantify these disparities is increasingly recognised, significant gaps persist in producing reliable, scalable, and actionable accessibility data. This study introduces a processing pipeline that leverages street-level imagery and deep learning techniques to estimate key characteristics of pedestrian sidewalk infrastructure — including sidewalk segmentation, surface type and condition detection, effective clearance estimation, and slope calculation — and synthesises them into an actionable dataset followed by a composite sidewalk wheelchair accessibility index (WHAI). The Wheelchair Accessibility Dataset (WHAD) pipeline combines monocular metric depth estimation, 3D point cloud reconstruction, and terrain model analysis to derive measurements from publicly available Mapillary street-level imagery. Results are validated against unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and aerial orthoimagery across areas of varying accessibility in Łódź, Poland, and Heidelberg, Germany. The resulting index enables the identification of localised inaccessible enclaves that conventional spatial analyses often overlook, providing an explainable and comparable foundation for evidence-based urban planning and improvements to pedestrian routing.",
